<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baganda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ganda people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baganda, or Ganda, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of central Uganda, the country's largest ethnic group, whose kingdom of Buganda lay on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>. In the late 19th century Ganda chiefs and soldiers allied with the British, fighting Bunyoro and serving as agents of colonial rule across the Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. Their language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luganda">Luganda</a>, is widely spoken in Uganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baganda.jpg" alt="Baganda" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Buganda.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Baganda share a culture, history, language and clan system and are primarily native to Buganda, a kingdom within Uganda. They are sometimes called the King's Men because of the importance of the king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a>, in their society, and number about 16 million in Uganda, with organised communities abroad. According to the most widely accepted account, Buganda was founded by Kato Kintu, a historical figure who took the name Kintu from the legend of the first man to establish his legitimacy, and who united scattered clans into a strong kingdom. In the creation myth Kintu married Nnambi, the daughter of the god Ggulu, and the Baganda are called the children of Nnambi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 18th century Buganda was eclipsing the formerly dominant kingdom of Bunyoro. Behind a centralised kingship the Baganda turned from defence to expansion, and by the middle of the 19th century Buganda had doubled and redoubled its territory, conquering much of Bunyoro. Conquered lands were put under chiefs nominated by the king, armies travelled on specially built roads, and a royal navy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe">outrigger canoes</a> under an admiral called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabunga">Gabunga</a> could carry commandos to any shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>.</p><p>The explorer John Speke visited Buganda in the 1860s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in 1875 found a well-ordered capital of about 80,000 people around the king's palace. In 1876 Christian missionaries began to enter the kingdom, and between 1881 and 1890 the Baganda began to convert to both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Every Muganda belongs to a clan, normally the father's; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> alone takes his mother's, so that every clan can produce a future king. The Baganda traditionally had 52 clans, of which 46 have been officially recognised since a 1993 survey. Each clan has a plant or animal totem that its members may not eat or harm, and members of one clan do not marry each other. Surnames depend on the clan and show whether the bearer is male or female.</p><p>Ekitiibwa, meaning honour or dignity, is the chief ideal. Unlike comparable notions in more stratified societies such as Rwanda, it was not conferred by birth, and all people openly competed for it. According to the 2002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, 42.7 per cent of Baganda are Roman Catholic, 27.4 per cent Anglican and 23 per cent Muslim, while some also follow the old Kiganda religion. They speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luganda">Luganda</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baganda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Because princes belonged to their mothers’ clans, the <b>Ganda</b> royal family had no clan of its own, and any clan could hope to provide a future kabaka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From late 1893, however, British forces under Colonel Henry Colvile, supported by large <b>Ganda</b> armies, invaded the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Uganda Protectorate the British placed Busoga under the <b>Ganda</b> general Semei Kakungulu in 1906, then created a council of chiefs and, in 1919, the office of Kyabazinga, chosen among the senior chiefs, to head Busoga as a single unit from Bugembe near Jinja.</blockquote><a href="/state/busoga-principalities">Busoga principalities</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain ruled indirectly, using <b>Ganda</b> chiefs as agents across the protectorate and preserving the kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
